{"uncertainty": 0.12, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause: uart_tx reloads its hold register on a start pulse while busy", "summary": "Requirement [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Inputs: the approved RTL [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1], whose review is [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.e1]; the VeriTriage session [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e1] / [run:run-0001], which classified the failure as unknown_failure at 30% confidence with top hypothesis 'Likely RTL bug'; and the triage reproduction [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2] / [run:run-0002], where 1 of 4 checks failed and $finish was reached at time 546000. DEFECT: the file's own header comment says 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The always block has an unconditional `if (start) hold <= data;` before the `case (state)`. That statement runs in every state, not only in IDLE. CAUSALITY: a start pulse during START, DATA or STOP overwrites `hold`. The DATA state reads `tx <= hold[bit_idx + 3'd1]` from the overwritten register, so the remaining data bits of the frame in flight come from the new byte. The faulty state is created at the cycle of the mid-frame start pulse, earlier than the receiver check that shows the failure. INCORRECTNESS: the specified behaviour is that the frame in flight is unaffected; only the IDLE branch should load `hold`, and that branch already does. The fix is to remove the statement outside the case, which also covers start pulses in the START and STOP states. HYPOTHESES, ranked: (1) rtl_bug, supported: the defect is visible in the source against its own stated behaviour. It would be refuted if the new repro below passed on this RTL. (2) testbench_bug, rejected for the new repro: its expected value (0xA5, the byte sent first) is derived from the header clause, not copied from RTL output. A clean frame's checks are included so that a broken receiver model would show up as failures there too. It would be refuted further if the repro failed on the RTL with the line removed; that run is not cited here. (3) infrastructure, disfavoured: the triage run built and reached $finish with a specific single check failing [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2], which is consistent with a functional failure, not a tool or build fault. A clean-rebuild experiment is not cited. (4) spec_ambiguity, rejected: the only clause available, the RTL header comment, states the busy-start behaviour explicitly. Caveat: no separate specification document is among my inputs, so the header comment is the reference used."}], "files": [{"path": "tb/uart_tx_reload_while_busy_repro_tb.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: start pulse while busy must not corrupt the frame in flight", "summary": "Self-checking repro for [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1], serving [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. It sends a clean 0x3C frame and checks its data and stop bit. It then sends 0xA5 and pulses start with 0x5A about 2.5 bit times into that frame. It checks that the received byte is still 0xA5, as the header clause specifies, that the stop bit is high, and that the line returns to idle. Expected values come from the stated behaviour. On the reload-while-busy RTL the 0xA5 check is expected to fail; on RTL that loads hold only in IDLE, all five checks are expected to pass. It prints one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict line with the run's nonce before $finish, and a watchdog covers a hang.", "entry": "uart_tx_reload_while_busy_repro_tb"}], "tool_runs": ["run:run-0001", "run:run-0002"], "claims": ["The new repro testbench is expected to fail exactly one check (frame 2 data) on the approved RTL and pass all checks on RTL without the out-of-case hold load; neither run has been performed by me.", "Removing the line `if (start) hold <= data;` outside the case statement is the proposed fix; it has not been simulated."], "escalation": null, "notes": "The gate's simulator.run (expect=fail) over the new testbench and the independent review record are platform/review steps still to come; nothing here is verified or approved by me. Receiver samples tx at negedge mid-bit (CLKS_PER_BIT=4), LSB first. 0xA5 and 0x5A differ in every bit, so any reloaded bit shows up as a mismatch."}

